---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modifications made for tts-ng
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The copy is upstream's `src/` at version 0.1.2 with the following changes.
The first five came out of the evaluation that decided on a copy rather than a
dependency; the sixth is a correctness fix found after the copy landed, and the
seventh is what wiring Chinese and Japanese up needed. Nothing else about the
normalization logic was touched.

1. `text_normalizer.rs`: added `FstTextNormalizer::from_bytes`, and removed
   `from_file`. `rustfst`'s `SerializableFst::load` takes the whole OpenFST
   binary as a `&[u8]`; upstream's `from_file` only reads a file into a buffer
   first. So the new constructor is that parse with the file read removed, which
   is the half `wasm32-unknown-unknown` cannot do.

2. `normalizer.rs`: the FST cache is no longer lazy. Upstream kept a
   `fst_dir` and read an FST the first time a configuration asked for it;
   here every FST arrives through `Normalizer::from_bytes` under the relative
   name upstream would have joined to that directory. `FstCache` and
   `get_or_load` are gone, upstream's `new(path)` / `with_defaults(path)` /
   free `normalize(path, text)` are gone with them, and `normalize` takes
   `&self` because there is nothing left to fill in. This is what makes the
   module usable where there is no filesystem at all, rather than merely
   compiling without one.

3. `normalizer.rs`: `full_to_half` moved from `postprocess` to `preprocess`.
   Upstream applies it after the TN, which is too late — `should_normalize`
   looks for an ASCII digit, and `２０２２年` has none, so full-width numerals
   reached the tagger unnormalised and the whole TN was skipped, silently.
   Measured on 11 Japanese inputs: 6/11 normalized upstream, 10/11 after the
   move. Japanese and Chinese text is full of full-width digits.

4. `contractions.rs`: the three embedded JSON paths are module-relative
   (`data/...`) rather than crate-relative (`../data/...`), because the file
   moved into `tn/wetext/`. `token_parser.rs`/`contractions.rs` import
   through `super::` for the same reason. No behaviour change.

5. `normalizer.rs`: `should_normalize` regained the `lang` parameter the port
   dropped, so that its digit test applies to every language *except* English —
   which is what the Python reference does. Without it the Rust port skipped the
   whole TN for an English sentence with no digit in it, which is the half of
   English TN that is not about digits: `Dr. Smith` is `doctor Smith` in the
   reference and came out unchanged here. Measured after the fix, the port agreed
   with the reference on 34 of 41 probe inputs. That count was taken **with
   modification 6 not yet made**, and the differences it counted as ties between
   readings of a bare digit string (`123` -> `one hundred and twenty three`
   there, `one two three` here) were in fact modification 6's bug: the two
   readings were not equally costly. The probe was a one-off against
   `pip install wetext==0.1.8` and is not committed, so this note does not claim
   a new number for it — only that the old one should not be read as a limit on
   how well the two agree now. See `README.md`.

6. `text_normalizer.rs`: the one-best path is no longer extracted with
   `rustfst::shortest_path`. That function does not handle **negative arc
   weights**, and these grammars are full of them — upstream ranks competing
   readings with `pynutil.add_weight(..., -0.0001)`, and a composed FST reaches
   `-0.0001` — so it returned paths that were not the cheapest. Measured on the
   composed FST for `cardinal { integer: "123" }`: seven readings exist, the
   cardinal `one hundred and twenty three` costs `0.000000`, `one two three`
   costs `0.000200`, and `shortest_path` returned the latter. Of 18 probe
   sentences through the real pipeline, 7 were read along a more expensive path.
   The copy now runs its own Bellman-Ford relaxation over the composed FST,
   records the arc each state was reached by, picks the final state minimizing
   `dist + final_weight` and walks the arcs back to the start. These grammars
   have no negative cycle (probed: 2-4 relaxation rounds, and the loop ends on a
   round that changes nothing). Same public API, same behaviour for an empty
   compose or an FST with no successful path — the input comes back unchanged.
   Measured on 3000 calls per input, the replacement is not slower than the
   `shortest_path` it replaces (best of five runs: 461 vs 466 us for
   `hello world`, 1273 vs 1289 us for `I have 123 apples.`, 1831 vs 1854 us for
   `Between 100 and 200 people.`). This is our change, not upstream's; upstream
   and `kaldifst` have the same defect.

Additionally, `error.rs` no longer has upstream's
`IoError(#[from] std::io::Error)` variant: it existed for the `std::fs`
constructors that modification 1 and 2 removed, and an unused `From<io::Error>`
is a way for an I/O error to arrive in a module that has no I/O.

The port had one pre-existing flaw that was *not* fixed, because nothing in
this crate reaches it: upstream's `fix_contractions` is off by default, and
the configuration this crate builds for English leaves it off.

7. `normalizer.rs`: `should_normalize` tests digits with
   [`char::is_numeric`](char::is_numeric) rather than `is_ascii_digit`. Upstream's
   line was a port of the Python reference's `re.search(r"\d", text)`, which over
   a Python `str` matches every character in Unicode general category `Nd` — and
   `is_ascii_digit` matches only `0-9`. `char::is_numeric` is `Nd | Nl | No`, a
   superset, so this can only make the normalizer *run* on text the reference
   would have skipped, which returns that text unchanged.

   The port's comment defended the narrower test on the grounds that the
   difference "does not reach English". That was true and beside the point: the
   branch in question is the one English never takes (`lang != En` below it), so
   the difference could only ever reach Chinese and Japanese — and it reached
   them everywhere. Fully half the numerals in real Chinese and Japanese text are
   written full-width (`２０２２年`, `１５．６％`), and `０` is U+FF10. Every one of
   those took the "nothing to normalize here" early return and came out as the
   digits it was written with, silently: dropping to the hand-written reader
   gives the same answer as not having this engine, so no test that only checked
   the output could tell the difference.

   Measured against `pip install wetext==0.1.8`: of 48 Chinese probe inputs the copy agreed on 45 before this change and
   47 after; of 29 Japanese inputs it agreed on 23 before and **29 after**. Every
   remaining disagreement is `1.2.3%`, a malformed number that the reference's
   chained-regex front end and this FST composition fragment differently, and that
   `crates/phonemize/tests/wetext_zh.rs` pins.

   Found by phase 9E, which is when Chinese and Japanese were wired up. Not
   reachable from English, so nothing between 9B and 9E could have noticed.

The port has one more limitation that is **not** ours, recorded in `README.md`:
`1000` is tagged `date { year: "1000" }`, where `ten hundred` and `one thousand`
are a genuine tie at `0.000100`. Modification 6 changed which non-minimal path
was returned; it did not change this, because here both readings *are* the
minimum.
